Collections form ordered, zero-indexed, homogeneous groups.

Collection types and literals use braces.

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    values ~= {1, 2, 3}
    names {String} = {"Priya", "Rob"}
]

### Literal inference

- A non-empty literal has one homogeneous element shape. Raw numeric origins in its entries can remain unresolved until eligible constraints across the function body select exact element types.
- Whole-number origins stay in the integer family. A non-empty literal with raw whole and decimal/exponent values selects the binary-float family in either item order. Whole values materialise directly at the selected float precision.
- A bound integer keeps its integer family. `{count, 2.5}` is rejected rather than reclassified.
- Every already typed item must match one exact element type. Arithmetic promotion does not search for a collection storage type. Convert differently typed items explicitly.
- Element type and fixed capacity remain stable through mutation.

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    accept_u64_values |items {U64}|:
    ;

    values = {1, 2, 3}
    accept_u64_values(values)

    first {F32} = {1, 2.5}
    second {F32} = {2.5, 1}

    count = 1
    -- Invalid: count keeps its integer family.
    mixed = {count, 2.5}
]

The same numeric-origin rules can pass through a known collection, map,
nominal/generic or optional shape when that shape already exists. They do not
infer an empty container or anonymous-record structure.

An empty literal needs an explicit collection type at its declaration or field
default site.

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    values ~{I32} = {}
]

The compiler does not infer an empty collection from:

- a later `push`
- later assignment
- a loop
- a function argument
- HIR use
- borrow analysis

### Collection versus map literals

A top-level key/value entry with `=` makes a non-empty brace literal a map
literal.

Every entry in that literal must then be a key/value pair.

Collection items and map entries cannot be mixed.

### Syntax boundaries

- Collections do not use square-bracket indexing.
- Square brackets belong to templates.
- Element reads and writes use compiler-owned collection members.
- `~` is binding or access mode. It is not part of collection type identity.

### Collection literal MON data boundary

Source and MON data share the empty-container distinction: `{}` is always a collection and `{=}` is always a map; a receiving type or schema cannot change the container's kind. Source `{=}` is accepted design but not implemented yet; see @../progress (Progress) for current support. See @../mon/#mon-format (MON format) for the Rust-only MON literal-data contract. Shared notation is owned by @../language-overview/#mon-syntax (MON syntax).
